Id: no-source-wire-name-outside-its-adapter
Pattern: "(first_air_date|original_name|original_title|poster_path|release_date|status_message|total_results|overview|results|vote_average|vote_count)"
Subject: *.cs
Except: :!Jellyfin.Plugin.Template/Sources/TmdbSourceAdapter.cs :!Jellyfin.Plugin.Template.Tests/TmdbSourceAdapterTests.cs :!Jellyfin.Plugin.Template.Tests/TitleRoundTripTests.cs
Issue: 73

A source's field names are that source's, and they stop at its adapter. The
interface in front of a source is written in this plugin's own types, so a
caller that reads `poster_path` has gone around it, and adding a second source
then means changing whoever did.

The pattern is the names the one adapter reads out of a response, quoted, which
is how a field name is spelled when it is used. Eleven of them rather than every
name in the response: `id`, `name`, `title` and `page` are words this codebase
uses for its own things, and a rule matching those would fire on ordinary work
for a reason its prose does not name. What is left is the vocabulary that could
only have come from this source, so a match is a caller reading the wire rather
than a coincidence.

The last two are the score and the count of scores, added when the adapter began
reading them under #91. A shelf's order is derived from what those two carried
onto the record, so the name a caller would reach for to sort a row by hand is
exactly the name that has to stop at the adapter.

That bound is real and this rule does not hide it. A caller reading `id` off a
response is doing the thing this rule is about and is invisible here. What
catches that instead is the interface: `IMetadataSource` answers in
`DiscoverTitle`, and a caller with no response in its hands has no `id` to read.

The other half of #73's second condition is a source's client library, and it
has no vocabulary because there is no such library. The adapter speaks to the
source directly, which #74 decided, and what holds it is
`Jellyfin.Plugin.Template.Tests/allowed-assembly-references.txt` read by
`AssemblyReferencesTests`: a reference the list does not name fails, so adding a
client library is a deliberate edit to that file rather than something that
arrives quietly. A pattern here could name nothing today, and a rule matching
nothing is one a reader counts.

Three exceptions, three paths, and the first two are the seam to a source. The
adapter is where the vocabulary belongs, and its own tests have to speak it to
assert what it maps.

The third is `TitleRoundTripTests.cs`, which is not an adapter's file and reads
the wire on purpose. It follows a title from a source's bytes out to a client
and back to the identifier it started with, so it reads the field a value came
from and compares it with the value that arrived; that is #55's assertion and it
cannot be made without naming both ends. It is one file with no convention to
derive it from, and naming it here is what stops the exception growing into a
list nobody can re-derive.

ALL THREE WERE FILENAME SUFFIXES UNTIL #387, AND THIS PARAGRAPH SEPARATED THE
THIRD FROM THE OTHER TWO ON EXACTLY THAT GROUND. The separation was not there to
be drawn. `*SourceAdapter.cs`, `*SourceAdapterTests.cs` and
`*TitleRoundTripTests.cs` are all names a later file is free to take, so a caller
reading the wire was one rename away from being outside this rule whatever it
read. The three differ in what they are for and never in their shape, and each of
them is now the path it meant.
